<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ulrich, Duke of Mecklenburg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1527–1603</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ulrich, Duke of Mecklenburg, was administrator of the Prince-Bishopric of Schwerin from 1550 and ruled Mecklenburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustrow">Güstrow</a> from 1555 until his death in 1603. A Lutheran prince, he introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> in the bishopric, which in practice became an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a> of the Mecklenburg house. He supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-rostock">University of Rostock</a> and rebuilt Güstrow Castle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> style. His daughter Sophia became queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ulrich-duke-of-mecklenburg.jpg" alt="Ulrich, Duke of Mecklenburg" width="320" height="549" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Theodor Fischer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ulrich3mecklenburg.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
